   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Just as Max celebrates winning Vika back, he walks out to the bar and is stunned to see Sasha standing there in uniform—she gave up her trip to Cyprus to stay in Moscow with him. Panicking because he already has plans with Vika tonight, Max tells Sasha he cannot see her because Kostya's beloved dog just died.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Костян, ты представляешь, это какой-то...",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "О, привет, Саш.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Спасибо, Костя, супер.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Ну, как видишь, нет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Хотела поговорить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Да, конечно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Что мы вместе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Ну, что, тогда до вечера?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Я, наверное, сегодня не смогу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Мне надо Костю поддержать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "А что случилось?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Да у него собака умерла.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Он ее очень любил.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Right, let's take their exchange slowly and unpack the Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Костян, ты представляешь, это какой-то...",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Kostyan, can you imagine, this is some kind of...' In Russian, the idea of 'Kostyan' flows naturally into 'kind of' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "О, привет, Саш.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Oh, hi, Sasha.' In Russian, the idea of 'about' flows naturally into 'Sasha' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Спасибо, Костя, супер.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thank you, Kostya, that is super.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'thank you' before landing on 'super'. Sasha answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Ну, как видишь, нет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, as you can see, I did not go.' The grammar packs 'come on' and 'there is no' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Хотела поговорить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I wanted to talk.' Kostya anchors the line on 'to want', while 'to talk' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Да, конечно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, of course.' Sasha brings out the contrast between 'yes' and 'of course' in a few short words. Kostya answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Что мы вместе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'That we are together.' The meaning hinges on 'what' working together with 'together' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Ну, что, тогда до вечера?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well then, see you tonight?' Sasha brings out the contrast between 'come on' and 'what' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Я, наверное, сегодня не смогу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I probably will not be able to make it tonight.' Notice how Russian expresses 'I' and 'will be able to' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Sasha answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Мне надо Костю поддержать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I need to support Kostya.' In Russian, the idea of 'for me' flows naturally into 'to support' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "А что случилось?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What happened?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'to happen'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Да у него собака умерла.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'His dog died.' Sasha brings out the contrast between 'yes' and 'by' in a few short words. Kostya answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Он ее очень любил.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
